  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nToday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nA widespread area of moderate rainfall is slowly making its way \nnorth and east out of the Central Plains and into the Mid/Upper \nMississippi River Valley. Heavier rainfall is just making it into \nNortheast Iowa and Southwest Wisconsin late overnight with the \nheaviest of the precipitation still back near the core of the low \naround Omaha, NE. That will trek NE through the remainder of the \novernight hours and through the morning. This will be co-located \nwith precipitable water values in the 1.6-1.9 inch range. The latest \n01.00 HREF 6 hour precipitation LPMM shows a few blotches of 3.5-4 \ninches across far southern portions of Fayette, Clayton, and Grant \nCounties. While there is still a 15-30% chance for 3-4 inches in \nthose aforementioned locations, more likely totals will be \ncloser to 2-2.5 inches. \n\nThere will be a fairly sharp northern cutoff in QPF with \nlocations outside of Northeast Iowa and Southwest Wisconsin \nlikely not seeing more than 0.5 inches of rain by 12Z Friday,\nevident in a sharp cutoff of precipitable water values in 01.03\nRAP analysis. PWATs drop off to less than 1.5 inches as one \nmoves north of the IA/MN state line with moisture transport \nmore prominent south of our area across Southern and Central \nIowa. This moisture transport will continue into northeastern \nIllinois and southern Wisconsin where the axis of heavy rain \nwill extend by morning. The heaviest rain should push off to the\neast by mid morning as a cold front starts to move in from the \nnorthwest. Rain chances will gradually end through the day with \nmost locations dry by 00Z Friday. \n\nThursday Night - Friday Night: Dry and Slightly Cooler \n\nHigh pressure is expected to build in behind the aforementioned cold \nfront. High temperatures Friday will be slightly cooler in the upper \n50s to low 60s with lows Thursday night in the upper 30s to upper \n40s. Friday night lows will drop into the mid 30s to mid 40s and \nsome places across central and north central Wisconsin could see \ntheir first areas of frost this fall. \n\nSaturday - Mid Next Week: Low Rain Chances Sat PM, Dry to Follow\n\nAnother cold front is expected to move southeast through the region \nSaturday afternoon/evening. Low potential for weak scattered \nshowers across portions of Southeast Minnesota and West Central \nWisconsin Saturday evening accompanying frontal passage but \noverall QPF amounts remain low, from 0.01-0.02 inches. This \nwould be a quick hitter and likely wouldn't even have much of an\nimpact on temperatures as highs Saturday and Sunday will be \ncomparable in the mid 60s. The only thing of note would be the \npotential for frost across portions of Central and North- \nCentral Wisconsin Monday morning as low temperatures could fall \ninto the mid-30s across that region. Otherwise, high pressure \nlooks to keep conditions dry next week with high temperatures \ngenerally in the 60s to near 70 degrees.",
